       Programs often need global or static variables that have different val‐
       ues in different threads.  Since threads share one memory  space,  this
       cannot be achieved with regular variables.  Thread‐specific data is the
       POSIX threads answer to this need.

       Each  thread possesses a private memory block, the thread‐specific data
       area, or TSD area for short.  This area is indexed by  TSD  keys.   The
       TSD  area  associates  values of type _v_o_i_d _* to TSD keys.  TSD keys are
       common to all threads, but the value associated with a  given  TSD  key
       can be different in each thread.

       For  concreteness,  the  TSD  areas  can  be viewed as arrays of _v_o_i_d _*
       pointers, TSD keys as integer indices into these arrays, and the  value
       of  a  TSD  key  as the value of the corresponding array element in the
       calling thread.

       When a thread is created, its TSD area initially associates  NULL  with
       all keys.

